group: Oh
formula: Cr(CO)6
construction: Fragment orbitals (extended Hückel)
headline: 
source: MO levels: RHF, def2-SVP · 
note: Model: order and approximate spacing from extended Hückel (qualitative) · calculation: eV, own scale · not drawn: (CO)6 internal: 22 levels, 44 e; 6 levels, empty; ligand π* levels: 6 levels, empty (Ligand-field view); tick Show all levels; untick Ligand-field view to draw them · electrons: Cr 6 (neutral: 4 unpaired e), drawn 6; (CO)6 60, drawn 22, internal 34 levels 38 e; model and calculation: 22 drawn + 44 folded (28 levels) = 66 · labels in O<sub>h</sub>\n4 high empty levels drawn at the top (marked with an up arrow); 6 very high empty levels not drawn; 3 levels match the calculation only loosely (overlap < 0.80)
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (Cr and (CO)6). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments, then paired per symmetry label and degeneracy, so each antibonding level has its bonding partner; a level without a partner is non-bonding. Colours mark the pairs.\n\n**Scale.** The model columns show the order of the levels and their approximate spacing from extended Hückel, not a scale: close levels are drawn further apart so they can be read, and no model energy is printed (it is in the hover text). Extended Hückel antibonding energies are qualitative and typically far too high for short bonds to diffuse atoms such as B, Be and Al.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in eV (not on the model's scale). For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. A level marked ≈ matches only loosely (overlap below 0.80; the value is in the hover text). Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe model explains your occupied orbitals in the right order. The order of some empty levels above the LUMO differs between the model and your calculation. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 66** (Cr 6, (CO)6 60).\n\nEach model level carries a short grey tag naming the fragment orbitals it is mostly made of (shares of at least 20 %), such as Cr d or CO π*. Ligand-field view hides empty ligand-centred levels such as CO π*; transitions into them are charge transfer, not d–d.\n\nThe levels counted in the note under the diagram are model levels that sit almost entirely on the (CO)6 and take no part in the interaction; they are counted, not drawn, in the calculation's column as well. The ns orbitals of a ligand atom that also has np (F 2s, Cl 3s) are treated as core-like here: the textbook sigma bonds are made of np.\n\n**Caveats.** 4 high empty levels drawn at the top (marked with an up arrow); 6 very high empty levels not drawn; 3 levels match the calculation only loosely (overlap < 0.80). Extended Hückel puts empty antibonding orbitals tens of eV up, so an empty level more than 30 eV above the HOMO is drawn at the top of the diagram, marked with an up arrow, or not at all when it sits almost entirely on one fragment. A loose match means no calculated orbital resembles the model level closely; read its calculated energy with care.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in Oh (cross-checked against the code's own D2H labels, which agreed on all 199 orbitals both sides could label, and every computed label subduces to match).
verdict: ok The model explains your occupied orbitals in the right order. Δo is 11.4 eV in the model and 17.4 eV in your calculation.
